  "anchors": {
    "Hook: What happens when you create an object without writing any setup code?": 0.0,
    "Question: Do I need to write an __init__ method for every class?": 6.081,
    "Concept: Python automatically provides a simple constructor called __init__ that runs whenever an instance is created, even if you never defined one.": 11.694,
    "Code: Here is a class with no __init__ defined, and we create an instance.": 21.986,
    "Execution: When we run the program, it prints the default representation of the object, showing that the hidden __init__ ran.": 28.534,
    "Takeaway: Even without writing one, Python gives each class a basic __init__.": 37.89,
    "Practice: Define a class called Account with no __init__, create an object, and print it.": 43.503
  },
